    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d764">

                <pb facs="1240" n="1226" xml:id="pg1226"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">356.115/12–2644: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Huddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-26T10:00:00+01:00">December 26,
                            1944—10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 13, 1945—5 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–876. American Interests—Netherlands—Germany. Legation’s airmail 9052 August
                        18.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d764-f1"/> Foreign Office note December 20 states
                    Swiss Legation Berlin received early in December note dated October 19 from
                    German Foreign Office reading in part in translation as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Foreign Office has ordered that personal property of British and
                            American nationals, as well as of nationals of other countries whose
                            interests are protected by Switzerland, will not be claimed for the use
                            of German people who have suffered damage through bombardment.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Huddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
